  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\qt0087_d133dcf5fd24.png\" style=\"width: 300px; height: 171px;\" /></p><p>The magnetic field at point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math>&nbsp;due to the two semi-infinite portions of both wires lying along line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi></math> will be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn></math>.</p><p>Using right-hand rule, it can be determined that the magnetic field due to both the semi-infinite portions of wires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>C</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>and</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>T</mi></math>&nbsp;which are perpendicular to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mi>E</mi></math> will be outwards from the plane.</p><p>The magnetic field due to one such portion will be:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mstyle></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p>Since the magnetic field of both the portions is in same direction, the net magnetic field at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>O</mi></math> will be:</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>e</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\"><mfenced><mfrac><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mfenced></mstyle></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>&#956;</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>I</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></mfrac></math></p>"
  }
}